// fpsmaze -- a first-person shooter in a randomly-generated maze, written in
// `id`, hardware-rendered via the OpenGL backend.
//
// A randomized-DFS maze (game/sim/maze) is carved fresh every run; you move
// through it in first person (WASD forward/back/strafe, Q/E turn, Space
// shoot) hunting (import num_targets) red cubes for score. See game/sim/actors for
// the player/target/input logic, game/sim/gfx for the GPU rendering, and
// idstd's fx_sin_deg/fx_cos_deg for the sine table `id` uses in place of a
// missing sin()/cos() builtin.
//
//   idc/bin/idc demos/fpsmaze --allow-untested -o fpsmaze && ./fpsmaze
//
// GFX_MAX_FRAMES lets a headless build self-terminate after N frames.

// main()'s no-display path: force_no_display (below) forces glwin_open() to
// fail the same way on every machine, whatever its own DISPLAY is, so ok=0
// and loop(ok) returns through spin's run<=0 branch without ever calling
// frame -- the one path that does not depend on GFX_MAX_FRAMES, an
// environment variable this build does not export. game_init's maze/target
// layout is seeded from the real clock (seed_rng, game/world/seed.id) so it
// is not reproducible here, but player_init's spawn (ps_x/ps_yaw) does not
// depend on that seed -- see game/sim/actors/player/state.id.
main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  int ok = glwin_open(800, 600, "id fpsmaze");
  game_init();
  loop(ok);
} return int 0;
given force_no_display (0, []):(0) then ps_x:(-12000) then ps_yaw:(0)
given force_no_display (0, []):(0) then calls frame:[0]

// Forces glwin_open() to fail deterministically -- on every machine, whatever
// its own DISPLAY is -- so main()'s no-display path can be cased. idstd's
// env_set (sys/io/ipc/env/write.id) reaches only this one case's own
// process (each case runs in a process of its own).
force_no_display() {
  int r = env_set("DISPLAY", "id-case-no-display");
} return void;
():() then display_probe:(18)
():() then env_error:(0)

// env_get's return is the value's exact byte length, or -1 if the name is
// not set at all -- so this is both "is DISPLAY forced" and "to exactly
// what", in one int. Dropping force_no_display's env_set call turns this
// from 18 into -1, not 0, so it cannot pass by coincidence.
display_probe() {
  int[] buf = [];
  int b = env_get("DISPLAY", buf, 0);
} return int b;
given force_no_display ():(18)
given force_no_display ():(18) then env_error:(0)
